To: Central Park Conservancy Research / Ecology Team
Subject: Partnership request: 30-day acoustic biodiversity pilot in Central Park
X-Delivery-Route: https://www.centralparknyc.org/contact-us

Hello Central Park Conservancy team,

My name is Rodrigo Castro, and I am developing Listening.bio, a working biodiversity-monitoring platform that turns environmental WAV recordings into traceable BirdNET candidate detections, human-reviewed observations, maps, and exportable evidence.

I am requesting a conversation about a small, permission-based Central Park pilot. The proposed scope is intentionally limited: three to five locations selected with your team, 50 to 100 short recordings over 30 days, and a conservative findings package that clearly separates model candidates from reviewed evidence.

The software is already functioning end to end. It supports real audio upload, configured BirdNET inference, source and model provenance, detection review, and CSV/GeoJSON exports. We have also completed a real public-audio validation run that correctly detected American Robin while preserving lower-confidence candidates for review.

Listening.bio would provide the technology workflow, processing, data organization, and pilot report. I am seeking Conservancy guidance on whether this could support an existing stewardship, research, or environmental-education priority; which locations and permissions would be appropriate; and what evidence would be useful to your team.

This would not be presented as a complete ecological survey. The objective is to test whether low-cost acoustic monitoring can responsibly complement existing work.

Would a 20-minute introductory conversation be possible? I can provide a live demo and one-page sampling protocol in advance.

Thank you,

Rodrigo Castro
